<% page_title "Privacy" %>
<% content_for :description, "What Omarchy HCL stores about a report, who can see it, and how to delete yours." %>

<article class="prose-omahcl max-w-2xl">
  <h1 class="mb-4 heading-page">Privacy</h1>
  <p>This site is a public list of hardware that runs Omarchy. Anyone running Omarchy can send a report of their machine, and the report is public once it arrives. The script shows what it would send and sends nothing unless you answer yes. Nobody needs an account. You can <a href="#deleting">delete your report</a> at any time.</p>

  <h2 class="mb-2 mt-8 heading-section">What a report holds</h2>
  <p>A report holds the vendor, model, product family and chassis type; the CPU model, vendor, architecture and core count; the memory size; up to eight GPU names; whether the machine boots with UEFI, has a TPM and has Secure Boot on; and the Omarchy version and release channel. It never holds serial numbers, MAC addresses, hostnames, usernames or product UUIDs. The script and the server both refuse those fields.</p>
  <p>The script also sends a one-way hash so that the same machine updates one report instead of adding new ones. It is computed on your machine from its machine-id, the model and the CPU. The machine-id is never sent, and the hash cannot be turned back into it. The server hashes it again with its secret key before storing it, so a copy of the database cannot be matched to your machine without that key.</p>

  <h2 class="mb-2 mt-8 heading-section">IP addresses</h2>
  <p>The site's own logs record which pages were requested, when, and the hardware facts in each submitted report. They do not record IP addresses, search terms or machine hashes.</p>
  <p>To limit abuse, the server counts report submissions, deletion requests and sign-in attempts per IP address over a few minutes. Those counts are deleted within about an hour. The site sleeps when nobody uses it, and counts left at that point are deleted within minutes of it waking up. Database backups keep the counts for up to a day after that.</p>
  <p>A report stores nothing derived from your IP address.</p>
  <p>The site runs on Laravel Cloud, behind Cloudflare. Both see your IP address when you connect and can keep it in their own logs. Laravel Cloud's access logs record each request's IP address, country, user agent and path. This site does not control those logs.</p>

  <h2 class="mb-2 mt-8 heading-section">Cookies</h2>
  <p>The site itself sets no cookies while you browse. Only the admin sign-in page and signed-in admins get session cookies. Cloudflare sets its own security cookies, which this site does not control. They include <code>__cf_bm</code>, which lasts 30 minutes, and <code>cf_clearance</code>, which holds the result of Cloudflare's bot check and lasts a year.</p>

  <h2 class="mb-2 mt-8 heading-section">Machines not running Omarchy</h2>
  <p>The script only reports machines running Omarchy. On any other system it says so and sends nothing, and the site never receives a report from such a machine. The site used to accept private "candidate" reports from these machines. It no longer does, and the ones it held have been deleted.</p>

  <h2 id="deleting" class="mb-2 mt-8 heading-section scroll-mt-6">Deleting your report</h2>
  <p>Run this in a terminal:</p>
  <div class="my-3"><%= render "application/collect_command", command: delete_command %></div>
  <p>It rebuilds your machine's hash, shows what it would send, and sends it only if you answer yes. The server deletes the report that matches it right away, including one an admin has hidden, and sends back how many it deleted. The hash is used for that and not stored. Database backups keep a deleted report for up to a day.</p>
  <p>The hash comes from your machine-id, model and CPU. If you have reinstalled, changed the model or CPU string (a BIOS update can do this), or reported before the hash dropped DMI serials on 19 October 2026, the script will not find your old report. There is no way to delete a report without those hashes.</p>

  <h2 class="mb-2 mt-8 heading-section">Who can see reports</h2>
  <p>Reports from machines running Omarchy are public, grouped by model. The home page also shows totals across all of them, such as machines per vendor, memory size and how many machines were added each week. Admins can see every report and can hide a report from public pages. Admin accounts store an email address and a password hash. Nobody else has an account.</p>
</article>
